The comment: "For every valid index i (from 0 up to the size of this list), the element at index i in the cloned list is the same as the element at index i in the original list."

We need to refer to the cloned list instance. In method clone(), we return Object (the cloned list). The assert should be about the result, i.e., \result is the cloned list. We need to use observer methods of the class: get(int index) and size(). Since we are inside the class, we can call this.get... for original list, and ((ArrayList<E>)\result).get... for cloned list. But we must use only publicly accessible observer methods, and calls should be of format this.func_name(args_list). For result, we can use \result.func_name? Not sure. Typically spec language: ((ArrayList<E>)\result).get(i). But they said use only publicly accessible observer methods provided in the test function. All function calls in the test function should be of the format this.func_name(args_list). However for result we may need to cast. In earlier examples they didn't need result usage. But spec may allow using \result as object and then calling its methods, like ((ArrayList<E>)\result).get(i). But they said "use \result instead of calling the observer method that is being tested". In this case we are testing clone, not get. So we can call get on both original and cloned.

Thus assert: \forall int i; 0 <= i && i < this.size(); ((ArrayList<E>)\result).get(i).equals(this.get(i));

